$DIR/main.p:17:9: cannot return h: it holds the function literal at main.p:16, which uses n, a local variable of Ptr
    A function literal that uses variables of the functions around it is a
    closure record, holding the code to run and where each variable is, in the
    frame of the function that makes it: here Ptr. That frame is reclaimed when
    Ptr returns, and the record with it.
    Ptr's results can hold the closure only through a pointer, so its callers
    provide no region to build it in (spec section 11.5). Return the closure
    itself, an interface holding it, or a struct or array holding it, instead.
    Go moves the captured variables and the closure to the heap. Plutonium never
    allocates behind the program's back, so the record lives in a frame: the
    maker's, or the region its caller provides.
    Return a value that carries its own state instead, such as a struct with a
    method:
        type state struct {
            n int
        }

        func (s *state) call() {
            ... s.n ...
        }
    (see "Closures and their regions" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.5)
    (run "pluto explain E3100" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:43:9: cannot return h: it holds the method value b.get (main.p:42), whose closure record is in PtrMethod's frame
    A method value such as b.get is a closure record, holding the method to call
    and a copy of the receiver, in the frame of the function that makes it: here
    PtrMethod. That frame is reclaimed when PtrMethod returns, and the record
    with it.
    PtrMethod's results can hold the closure only through a pointer, so its
    callers provide no region to build it in (spec section 11.5). Return the
    closure itself, an interface holding it, or a struct or array holding it,
    instead.
    Go moves the captured variables and the closure to the heap. Plutonium never
    allocates behind the program's back, so the record lives in a frame: the
    maker's, or the region its caller provides.
    Return the receiver and let the caller call the method, or return a method
    expression, which takes the receiver as its first argument:
        m := (*box).get // m(x, ...) calls x.get(...)
    (see "Closures and their regions" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.5)
    (run "pluto explain E3100" for a longer explanation)

